WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.700
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:09.169
Today, we have an abundance of neural network-based
image generation techniques.

00:00:09.169 --> 00:00:14.330
Every image that you see here and throughout
this video is generated by one of these learning-based

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:15.429
methods.

00:00:15.429 --> 00:00:20.609
These can offer high-fidelity synthesis, and
not only that, but we can often even exert

00:00:20.609 --> 00:00:23.230
artistic control over the outputs.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:25.710
We can truly do so much with these.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:29.910
And if you are wondering, there is a reason
why we will be talking about an exact set

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:33.510
of techniques, and you will see that in a
moment.

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:38.300
So the first one is a very capable technique
by the name CycleGAN!

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:44.449
This was great at image translation, or in
other words, transforming apples into oranges,

00:00:44.449 --> 00:00:47.540
zebras into horses, and more.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:53.199
It was called CycleGAN because it introduced
a cycle consistency loss function.

00:00:53.199 --> 00:00:58.769
This means that if we convert a summer image
to a winter image, and then back to a summer

00:00:58.769 --> 00:01:02.359
image, we should get the same input image
back.

00:01:02.359 --> 00:01:07.630
If our learning system obeys to this principle,
the output quality of the translation is going

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.630
to be significantly better.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:15.400
Later, a technique by the name BigGAN appeared,
which was able to create reasonably high quality

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:22.370
images and not only that, but it also gave
us a little artistic control over the outputs.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:28.660
After that, StyleGAN and even its second version
appeared, which, among many other crazy good

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:34.920
features, opened up the possibility to lock
in several aspects of these images, for instance,

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:41.470
age, pose, some facial features and more,
and then, we could mix them with other images

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:45.689
to our liking, while retaining these locked-in
aspects.

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:50.159
And of course, DeepFake creation provides
fertile grounds for research works, so much

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:56.060
so that at this point, it seems to be a subfield
of its own where the rate of progress is just

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:57.259
stunning.

00:01:57.259 --> 00:02:01.730
Now that we can generate arbitrarily many
beautiful images with these learning algorithms,

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:07.729
they will inevitably appear in many corners
of the internet, so an important new question

00:02:07.729 --> 00:02:12.240
arises - can we detect if an image was made
by these methods?

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.720
This new paper argues that the answer is a
resounding yes.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.730
You see a bunch of synthetic images above,
and real images below here, and if you look

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:27.730
carefully for the labels, you’ll see many
names that ring a bell to our Scholarly minds.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:32.160
CycleGAN, BigGAN, StyleGAN…nice!

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.500
And now, you know that this is exactly why
we briefly went through what these techniques

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.510
do at the start of the video.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:42.580
So, all of these can be detected by this new
method.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:48.240
And now, hold on to your papers, because I
kind of expected that, but what I didn’t

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:53.250
expect is that this detector was trained on
only one of these techniques, and leaning

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:57.110
on that knowledge, it was able to catch all
the others!

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.340
Now that’s incredible.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:04.530
This means that there are foundational elements
that bind together all of these techniques.

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:08.942
Our seasoned Fellow Scholars know that this
similarity is none other than the that the

00:03:08.942 --> 00:03:12.950
fact that they are all built on convolutional
neural networks.

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:17.490
They are vastly different, but they use very
similar building blocks.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:22.340
Imagine the convolutional layers as lego pieces,
and think of the techniques themselves to

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.220
be the objects that we build using them.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:30.690
We can build anything, but what binds these
all together is that they are all but a collection

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.240
of lego pieces.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:38.620
So, this detector was only trained on real
images and synthetic ones created by the ProGAN

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:43.240
technique, and you see with the blue bars
that the detection ratio is quite close to

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:47.570
perfect for a number of techniques, save for
these two.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:50.530
The AP label means average precision.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:54.580
If you look at the paper in the description,
you will get a lot more insights as to how

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:59.590
robust it is against compression artifacts,
a little frequency analysis of the different

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:01.760
synthesis techniques, and more.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.430
Let’s send a huge thank you to the authors
of the paper, who also provide the source

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:09.320
code and training data for this technique.

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:13.940
For now, we can all breathe a sigh of relief
that there are proper detection tools that

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:16.190
we can train ourselves at home.

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:19.660
In fact, you will see such an example in a
second.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:22.250
What a time to be alive!

